\subsection{Both conics are singular}

In this section we consider polygonal lines inscribed in a singular conic and circumscribed about another one.
The circumscribed conic $\Gamma$ is then a pair of lines $g_1$, $g_2$, while the inscribed conic $\mathcal{C}$ is represented by a pair of points $C_1$, $C_2$.

\begin{example}\label{ex:harmonic}
It is well known that four collinear points $(X, Y, Z, W)$ are harmonically conjugate if and only if there is a quadrangle $ABCD$ such that $X=AB\cap CD$, $Y=AD\cap BC$, $Z=BD\cap XY$, $W=AC\cap XY$. 
The construction does not depend on the choice of the quadrangle and this follows from the Desargues theorem, see e.g.~\cite{Wylie}.
Using this construction of harmonic conjugacy via quadrangles, one can see that, if the lines $g_1$, $g_2$ intersect $C_1C_2$ in a pair of points $(D_1, D_2)$, which is harmonically conjugated with the pair $C_1$, $C_2$, then there will be a family of infinitely many quadrangles inscribed in $\Gamma$ and circumscribed about $\mathcal{C}$, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:harmonic}. Let us recall also that the cross-ratio of the harmonically-conjugated points is equal to $-1$. %\sout{Moreover, the same will be true for any other singular conic $\Gamma'$ which consists of a pair of lines $g_1', g_2'$, such that $g_1'$ contains $D_1$ and $g_2'$ contains $D_2$.}
\end{example}
