The direct proton removal cross sections, $\sigma_{\text{cc}}^{\text{direct}}$, is calculated with the zero-range optical-limit approximation Glauber model (ZRGM)~\cite{MengJ2002,Bhagwat2004}. In the case of $^{\text{nat}}$C target, $^{12}$C is used in the calculation instead of the $^{\text{nat}}$C as it is predominantly (approximately 99\%) composed of $^{12}$C. Proton and neutron density distributions based on the Harmonic-oscillator (HO) model are employed for both $^{12}$C and $^{18}$O. 
\begin{equation}\label{eq20}
\rho(r) = \rho_{0}\left[1+\frac{C-2}{3}\left(\frac{r}{\omega}\right)^{2}\right]exp\left[-\left(\frac{r}{\omega}\right)^{2}\right],
\end{equation}
where $\rho_{0}$ denotes the central density, $C$ represents the number of protons or neutrons, and $\omega$ is the radius parameter. The relevant density distributions are determined by reproducing the experimental charge radii~\cite{Angeli2013} and the interaction cross sections on carbon at around 1000 MeV/nucleon~\cite{Ozawa2001}. Detailed parameters used in the nucleon density distributions of $^{12}$C and $^{18}$O are listed in Table~\ref{tab1}.

