Climate models are crucial for attribution studies. When large anomalies occur, e.g. a particularly hot year, climate scientists typically perturb the inputs to ESMs and observe any changes in the projected climate to attribute causal drivers to these events \citep{hiatus_kosaka}. This is compute- and time-consuming, particularly if many experiments are needed to capture the probabilistic nature of the simulations. With \name{}, due to its causal interpretability, we can directly intervene on our learned causal graph to carry out counterfactual experiments. 

