Th first problem about the matrix $A_n$ we would like to study is:
 are the matrices $A_n$ invertible, i.e. $\det A_n\neq 0$, asymptotically almost surely? This problem can be studied within two levels of generality, (I) when we assume that the nonzero entries among $a_{ij}$ have a bounded density, or (II) when we make no density assumption, say when the nonzero entries among $a_{ij}$ are uniformly distributed on $\{\pm 1\}$. The case (I) is already interesting in its own right, since only very few entries (say, those near the diagonal) have a density and all other entries are set to be zero, so a standard argument does not apply. To our best knowledge, no such results on invertibility with high probability was previously known, even with a density assumption. The case (II) is even more fascinating, as it serves as a far-reaching generalization of the well-known problem of whether a random matrix with independent $\{\pm 1\}$ entries is nonsingular with high probability. For the latter problem, the first proof that such matrix is invertible with probability $1-o(1)$ was given in Komlós \cite{komlos1967determinant}, and the singularity probability of the matrix was intensively studied in \cite{kahn1995probability}, \cite{tao2007singularity}, \cite{bourgain2010singularity},\cite{tikhomirov2020singularity}. However, it is widely perceived that the results and techniques in the study of singularity probability of $A_n$ with all entries being i.i.d., does not neatly generalize to the case when $A_n$ has $o(n)$ number of nonzero entries per row/column.



 The first work on circular law for random band matrices with $o(n)$ bandwidth was by Jain,Jana,Luh and O’Rourke \cite{jain2021circular} who studied a block band matrix model. The particular block structure of the model in \cite{jain2021circular} enabled them to prove invertibility of $A_n$ with high probability and prove convergence of $\mu_n$ to the circular law for certain regimes of the bandwidth, without making any density assumption on entries of $A_n$. The particular block structure of the model played an essential role in the proof of \cite{jain2021circular}, and thus the proof does not apply to more general versions of non-Hermitian random band matrix. Later, Tikhomirov \cite{tikhomirov2023pseudospectrum} introduced a method to bound the least singular values of $A_n-z\mathbf{1}$ when $z$ is bounded away from 0 in a quantitative way, with the advantage that no specific structure is assumed on the variance profiles of $A_n$ but require that the nonzero entries of $A_n$ have a bounded density. As a corollary, convergence to the circular law was proven in \cite{tikhomirov2023pseudospectrum} for a much wider class of models beyond the block band matrix. The argument of \cite{tikhomirov2023pseudospectrum} also does not imply invertibility of $A_n$ with high probability, even assuming a bounded density. The quantitative estimates on least singular values in \cite{tikhomirov2023pseudospectrum} and \cite{jain2021circular}  have a similar magnitude, despite their assumptions being largely non-overlapping. Recently, \cite{han2024circular} improved the convergence results to the circular law in \cite{tikhomirov2023pseudospectrum} and \cite{jain2021circular}, allowing for a much smaller bandwidth (sparser variance profile) by improving the estimates on intermediately small singular values. 

 In \cite{tikhomirov2023pseudospectrum}, remark 3.6, Tikhomirov proposed an open problem on whether the least singular value bounds in  \cite{tikhomirov2023pseudospectrum} can be obtained without assuming a bounded density. The question of nonsingularity of $A_n$, as discussed in previous paragraphs, is also open at this level of generality. In this paper we introduce a very general family of matrix models where both problems can be given an affirmative answer, and the model includes the most natural versions of non-Hermitian random band matrices
one can think of. We maintain a moderate extent of generality, going far beyond the block band matrix model, but also assume that there are a sufficient number of random potentials in a structured region across the main diagonal. 



\begin{Definition}\label{definition131}
    Consider a random matrix $A_n=V\odot W$, where $V$ is a $n\times n$ deterministic matrix with non-negative entries, and $W$ is an $n\times n$ random matrix with real or complex entries. The symbol $\odot$ denotes entrywise product, or Hadamard product, so that $a_{ij}=v_{ij}w_{ij}$ where we write $A=(a_{ij}),B=(b_{ij})$, $W=(w_{ij})$. 


    Let $d_n\in\mathbb{N}$ be a sequence of integers with $1\leq d_n\leq n$. We assume the following conditions on the randomness $W$:
        \begin{enumerate}
        \item The entries $w_{ij}$ of $W$ are independent random variables with mean 0, variance 1. 
        \item The entries  $w_{ij}$ of $W$ are uniformly anticoncentrated in the following sense: we can find some $\beta\in(0,1)$ such that 
        \begin{enumerate}\item When $w_{ij}$ is real-valued, then $\mathcal{L}(w_{ij},1)\leq \beta$ for each $i,j=1,\cdots,n$. \item  When $w_{ij}$ are complex valued, then its real and complex parts, denoted by $w_{ij}^\Re,w_{ij}^\Im$, are independent random variables satisfying that $\mathcal{L}(w_{ij}^\Re,\frac{1}{2})\leq \beta$ and $\mathcal{L}(w_{ij}^\Im,\frac{1}{2})\leq \beta$ for each $i,j=1,\cdots,n$.
        \end{enumerate}
    \end{enumerate}


    We assume the following conditions on the variance $V$: we can find positive constants $C_V^1,C_V^2,C_V^3$ independent of $n$ such that 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The entries close to diagonal are bounded from below and above:
        $$
C_V^1\leq v_{ij}\leq C_V^2\quad \forall 1\leq i,j\leq n:|i-j|\leq d_n.
        $$
        \item Bounded norm for each row: for each $i=1,\cdots,n$ we have
        $$\sum_{j=1}^n v_{ij}^2\leq C_V^3 \cdot d_n.
        $$
    \end{enumerate}

\end{Definition}

The assumptions (1), (2) on $V$ we impose here imply that there is a belt of length $\Theta(d_n)$ along the main diagonal such that the random potentials close to this belt are uniformly anti-concentrated. Away from this belt, there are not too many other random variables, but our definition is general enough so that these random variables away from the belt can lie in any possible place. This offers a lot of flexibility to the variance profile $V$.

In our assumptions on the random variables $w_{ij}$ in $W$, we make no hypothesis on the higher moments of $w_{ij}$ beyond the second moment, so we typically do not have a control of $\|A_n\|_{op}$. This can be contrasted with \cite{jain2021circular} which assumed finite $4+\epsilon$ moments and \cite{tikhomirov2023pseudospectrum} which assumed a subgaussian moment: both these assumptions are crucial in their proof but is unnecessary in this paper. Also, we do not assume that $w_{ij}$ are identically distributed: what we need is only that they have a uniform anti-concentration function.

For some results, we need to assume in addition that $w_{ij}$ has a bounded density:

\begin{Assumption}\label{assumptiondensity} When we assume that entries of $W$ have a bounded density, this means that we can find a constant $\rho_0>0$ such that \begin{enumerate}
    \item Either $w_{ij}$ is a real random variable with density bounded by $\rho_0$,
    \item Or the real and imaginary parts of $w_{ij}$, denoted by $w_{ij}^\Re,w_{ij}^\Im$, are independent random variables with density bounded by $\rho_0$. 


\end{enumerate}
    This assumption trivially implies assumption 2(b) of Definition \ref{definition131}.
\end{Assumption}


\subsection{Invertibility of the band matrix}

The main results on invertibility of $A_n$ is as follows. 
\begin{theorem}(Invertibility, general distribution)
    Let $A_n$ be the random matrix defined in Definition \ref{definition131}. Assume that 
    $$
\sqrt{n}\leq d_n\leq n.
    $$Then 
      when $n\geq n_0$, we have 
\begin{enumerate}\item  $A_n$ is ivertible with probability $1-o(1)$:  $$
\mathbb{P}(\det A_n\neq 0)\geq 1-\frac{C}{d_n},
    $$ where $n_0\in\mathbb{N}_+$ and $C>0$ depends  only on the parameters in Definition \ref{definition131}. \item When $A_n$ is real-valued, we further verify that 
    $$
\mathbb{P}(\sigma_{min}(A_n)\geq\exp(-\frac{n}{d_n}))\geq 1-\frac{C}{d_n}.
    $$\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

When the entries of $W_n$ have a density, we can go down to $d_n\ll\sqrt{n}$:


\begin{theorem}(Invertibility, bounded density) Let $A_n$ be the random matrix defined in Definition \ref{definition131}, which furthermore satisfies Assumption  \ref{assumptiondensity}. Then for any $1\leq d_n\leq n$,
\begin{enumerate}
\item We have
$$
\mathbb{P}(\det A_n\neq 0)\geq 1-e^{-Cn}. 
$$ \item 
 When $A_n$ is real valued, we further prove that for any $\epsilon>0$,
 $$
\mathbb{P}(\sigma_{min}(A_n)\leq \epsilon\exp(-\frac{n}{d_n}))\leq C\epsilon+e^{-Cn}.
 $$ \item 
 When the entries of $W_n$ are bounded, i.e., when we can find $M>0$ such that $|w_{ij}|\leq M$ almost surely for each $i,j$, then we have 
 $$
\mathbb{P}(\det A_n\neq 0)=1.
 $$\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

