      \field{abstract}{We introduce the notion of \$\textbackslash epsilon\$-irreducibility for arithmetic cycles meaning that the degree of its analytic part is small compared to the degree of its irreducible classical part. We will show that for every \$\textbackslash epsilon{$>$}0\$ any sufficiently high tensor power of an arithmetically ample hermitian line bundle can be represented by an \$\textbackslash epsilon\$-irreducible arithmetic divisor. Our methods of proof also allow us to study the distribution of divisors of small sections of an arithmetically ample hermitian line bundle \$\textbackslash overline\{\textbackslash mathcal\{L\}\}\$. We will prove that for increasing tensor powers \$\textbackslash overline\{\textbackslash mathcal\{L\}\}\textasciicircum\{\textbackslash otimes n\}\$ the normalized Dirac measures of these divisors almost always converge to \$c\_1(\textbackslash overline\{\textbackslash mathcal\{L\}\})\$ in the weak sense. Using geometry of numbers we will deduce this result from a distribution result on divisors of random sections of positive line bundles in complex analysis. As an application, we will give a new equidistribution result for the zero sets of integer polynomials. Finally, we will express the arithmetic intersection number of arithmetically ample hermitian line bundles as a limit of classical geometric intersection numbers over the finite fibers.}
