\subsection{Plan quality}% and robustness}
\autoref{tb:dosimetric} demonstrates the quantitative dosimetric performance comparison, including D\textsubscript{98}, D\textsubscript{2}, CI, HI, brainstem mean and max, of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} and SPArc\textsubscript{ps} on nominal distribution. With regard to CTV, SPArc\textsubscript{dl} plan presents a significantly ($p < 0.01$) smaller average HI compared to the SPArc\textsubscript{ps} with average differences of $0.71$. The D\textsubscript{2} values are close between the two plans with SPArc\textsubscript{dl} plan displaying a lower value, and this difference is statistically significant ($p < 0.01$). These results indicate SPArc\textsubscript{dl} can provide a more homogeneous dose distribution. Furthermore, SPArc\textsubscript{dl} exhibits a higher CI value, differing by $0.16$ compared to SPArc\textsubscript{ps}. The CI difference is significant ($p < 0.01$), reflecting better conformity of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} to the target. In respect to brainstem, SPArc\textsubscript{dl} yields a lower mean and max dose, differing by $0.21$ and $0.15$. The mean and max difference are both statistically significant ($p < 0.01$), demonstrating better sparing of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} on this critical structure.

A more intuitive performance comparison of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} (blue triangle) and SPArc\textsubscript{ps} (red squares) plans is demonstrated in \autoref{fig:gtv_metrics_comparison}, in which the distribution of CI, HI, D\textsubscript{98}, D\textsubscript{2}, mean and max dose of brainstem are presented as a function of GTV using kernel density estimates and scatter plots. In plot (a), the HI values of SPArc\textsubscript{ps} plan range from about $0.2$ to $2.5$, with a dense cluster around 0.2-1, while those of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} plan are lower and with a tighter cluster, mostly between $0.1$ and $0.3$ suggesting improved homogeneity of the proposed method. Also in plot (c), the D\textsubscript{98} values of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} plan are higher, mostly between $1.5$ and $1.8$, with a dense cluster around 1.6-1.7, indicating better minimum dose coverage. The CI values of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} are higher in plot (b), mostly between $0.6$ and $0.8$, with a dense cluster around the same range, showing better conformity to the target volume. The observations and findings of \autoref{fig:gtv_metrics_comparison} align with the insights of \autoref{tb:dosimetric}. In all four plots, the relatively denser cluster generated by SPArc\textsubscript{dl} plan indicates greater consistency of its performance. 

The denser clustering and reduced variance observed in SPArc\textsubscript{dl}'s results in \autoref{fig:gtv_metrics_comparison} demonstrate its superior capacity to generate consistent outcomes compared to SPArc\textsubscript{ps} in EL pre-selection.

%\textcolor{red}{Need a paragraph to describe the result of robustness evaluation ...}

\begin{figure}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{diagram/gtv_compare_3x2.pdf}
    \caption{Comparison of various metrics performance of $54$ cases between SPArc\textsubscript{dl} and SPArc\textsubscript{ps} plans. Each diagram shows the relationship between GTV volume and the respective metric, displaying kernel density estimate (KDE) plots and scatter points for both methods.}
    \label{fig:gtv_metrics_comparison}
\end{figure}

A comparable plan in a previous study \cite{ma2024machine} reported low dose conformity index with respect to small lung tumor sizes. Although the primary focus of this study is NPC, we are interested in checking if we have the similar finding. As demonstrated in \autoref{fig:gtv_metrics_comparison} (b), the CI exhibits modest decline as the GTV volume reduces from approximately $100$ to $20$ cc. In short, SPArc\textsubscript{dl} demonstrates a slightly lower CI for relatively small NPC GTV volume. 


\subsection{EL switch time}
The performance of the model is evaluated in terms of mean energy layer switch (ELS) time in \autoref{el_jump_switch_time}. unchanged ELS refers to the scenario in which the EL remains unchanged when the beam angle transitions to the next position. In such cases, the ELS time is effectively zero, which is shorter than that required for both ELS upward or downward transitions. \autoref{el_jump_switch_time} presents the mean ELS associated with ascended, descended and unchanged transition of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} and SPArc\textsubscript{ps}. The mean unchanged ELS of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} is $52.6$, which is dramatically higher than that of SPArc\textsubscript{ps} or $0.3$. Compared to SPArc\textsubscript{ps}, the number of descended ELS of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} is almost entirely shifted to the unchanged ELS. While the mean ascended ELS of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} is slightly higher than that of SPArc\textsubscript{ps}, the notable advantage of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} lies in its higher frequency of unchanged ELS. This advantage not only offsets the the cost of slightly higher EL in ascended transitions but also contributes to energy switch time of $51.9$ seconds, an $38.4\%$ reduction from SPArc\textsubscript{ps}. In addition, \autoref{fig:angle_energy} demonstrates a few cases of EL generated by SPArc\textsubscript{dl}, where many unchanged ELS can be observed. 

