\subsection{Model Efficiency}
The mean inference time of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} for generating ELs pre-selection during SPArc plan optimization is $0.05$ seconds on a CPU, representing a significant improvement in speed compared to the performance of the previous method~\cite{qian2023novel}, which is $16$ seconds ($p<0.01$). This enhanced efficiency is primarily due to SPArc\textsubscript{dl}'s capability to simultaneously model the spot-organ and spot-target information across all gantry angles and produce EL pre-selection in a single forward pass. 

\section{Discussion}
In this study, we proposed SPArc\textsubscript{dl}, a deep learning method to rapidly pre-select EL that maintain high treatment quality and significantly reduce ELS time in PAT, without relying on supervised training data. The proposed method is tested on $54$ NPC patient cases and evaluated against a published method SPArc\textsubscript{ps}. 

The data preprocessing involves matrices creation of $M_{CTV}$ and $M_{CTV-BRS}$, in which $M_{CTV}$ represents the number of proton spot intersecting the CTV and $M_{CTV-BRS}$ indicates the spots intersecting CTV but not the brainstem. The values of both both matrices are structured by gantry angles and EL. %SPArc\textsubscript{dl} is based on our assumption that these matrices serves as proxies for radiation coverage. 
SPArc\textsubscript{dl} takes in these matrices and WET vectors to generate the optimal EL of all gantry angles. A tri-objective loss function is designed to encourage the number of spots on CTV, penalize the spots on brainstem and discourage time-consuming ELS. The plan quality of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} is comparable or better than that of SPArc\textsubscript{ps}, and ELS time has been largely improved. 

The contributions of this study is as follows. (1) To the best of our knowledge, the proposed SPArc\textsubscript{dl} is the first deep learning model solving EL pre-selection problem for PAT plan optimization. Compared to SPArc\textsubscript{ps}, SPArc\textsubscript{dl} demonstrates comparable or superior dosimetric performance, including dose conformity, homogeneity, and OAR sparing, while achieving greater consistency in results. (2) The experimental results of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} unintentionally revealed that deploying high frequency of unchanged ELS can be more time-wise efficient than high frequency of descended ELS. SPArc\textsubscript{dl} achieves improvements in delivery efficiency by leveraging unchanged ELS to significantly reduce ELS time. (3) This study introduces a novel data representation, termed spot-count representation, which is used as a proxy for dose coverage, enabling effective learning without direct dose calculations. Spot-count representation establishes an avenue to employ deep learning to the task of EL pre-selection. %The efficacy of the spot-count representation is validated by the strong performance of SPArc\textsubscript{dl}. 
(4) The unsupervised approach of this study not only eliminates the complications associated with ground truth generation, but also leaves objective function open-ended where multiple objectives can be designed and simultaneously optimized, paving the way to deep learning-based multi-criteria optimization of SPArc plan optimization.
(5) SPArc\textsubscript{dl} enhances model inference speed by generating optimal EL sequences within 1 second, supporting fast clinical decision-making and planning workflows. %(6) The efficacy of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} is validated by 54 test cases, a relatively large testing dataset compared to 3 and 9 testing cases in the previous works~\cite{qian2023novel, ma2024machine}. 

%\textcolor{red}{Limitation. The DVH band robustness generated by SPArc\textsubscript{dl} is a limitation.} 
One limitation of SPArc\textsubscript{dl} is the instability of its predictions, which is largely attributed to the complexity involved in tuning the loss weight parameters. Future work will focus on developing an automated approach to replace the current manual and time-consuming process of objective weight tuning. Another limitation of this study stems from computational resource constraints on the research platform, which necessitated sampling the full-arc gantry angles at 5-degree intervals. We plan to enhance computational capacity to enable the analysis of a greater number of gantry angles in future investigations. Additionally, the current approach only incorporates spot counts for the clinical target volume (CTV) and brainstem, along with the mean water equivalent thickness (WET) value for each angle. Other potentially informative features—such as beamlet-level characteristics, geometric parameters, and a broader range of WET-related features~\cite{ma2024machine}—were not considered. Incorporating these features as model inputs may lead to improved performance. % SPArc\textsubscript{dl} uses U-Net, a classic deep learning architecture as backbone, where more advanced model architecture can be employed to enhance performance. 

\section{Conclusion}
The proposed SPArc\textsubscript{dl} is able to achieve comparable or superior plan quality for delivery efficient proton arc therapy plan optimization, compared to the previous work SPArc\textsubscript{ps}. The proposed spot-count representation establishes a foundation for unsupervised deep learning application in EL pre-selection of PAT plan optimization. We hope this work can inspire more deep learning-based methods in this field. 



