\begin{abstract}
Access to large amounts of high-quality labeled data is often a limiting factor in applied supervised learning regimes.  
Active learning (AL) is a popular strategy to improve model performance by iteratively making an informed selection of instances from an unlabeled data pool for annotation.
However, traditional AL frameworks largely neglect Human Label Variation (HLV) and rest on assumptions that often do not hold in practice, especially in linguistic annotation: (A1) there exists a single ground truth label for each instance; (A2) an omniscient oracle provides these labels; and (A3) annotation costs are the same for all instances. In natural language processing tasks, plausible human label variation exists, while annotators may additionally provide noisy labels. 
Moreover, the increasing use of large language models (LLMs) as annotators further challenges the assumption of equal annotation costs and quality. 
In this paper, we revisit the classical AL loop, focusing on necessary adaptations to acknowledge HLV and incorporate LLMs. We advocate for explicitly addressing assumptions about the nature of the ground truth labels to better interpret observed label variation. This work aims to propose refinements of AL strategies, making them more robust and reflective of real-world linguistic annotation challenges.
\end{abstract}
