In the concave curve, Y tends to zero when T tends to zero or infinity, so to define a minimum and maximum temperature, it is necessary to define a minimum Y, then the intercepts of the curve with the minimum Y would be the minimum and maximum temperatures.
(B) Convex response with a characteristic optimum that corresponds to a minimum. In the convex curve, when T tends to zero, there is an intercept with the Y axis (which is not the maximum), and T tends to infinity, Y tends to infinity too. The insets show the curve in the $ln(Y)-(1/T)$ space, which is the easier way to fit the data into the model.}
\label{fig1}
\end{figure}

\begin{framed}

\textbf{Box 2. Scaling behavior in thermal performances.} Here, with "scaling behavior" we refer to some quantity $u$ that changes with a quantity $v$ according to $u=u_0 v^w$. Our theory predicts two classes of scaling behavior;

1) In the general equation, in the limit $\Delta H \rightarrow 0$ it is predicted that 

\begin{equation}\label{powerlawdecay}
Y(T)\approx Y_0 T^{\Delta H+ \alpha R}
\end{equation}

2) Among different curves, e.g., of a given response variable, such as population growth rate, for different species, there are emerging relationships defined by the analytic form of the thermal traits. These relationships can be predicted, for example, from the general equation that includes body size for an optimal temperature, $Y(T_{opt})=Y_0 M^{\beta}e^{-\Delta H/T_{opt}}T_{opt}^{-\Delta C-R\alpha})$, but $T_{opt}=\frac{-\Delta H}{\Delta C-R\alpha}$, then

\begin{equation}
Y(T_{opt})=Y_0 M^{\beta}e^{-\Delta C-R\alpha} T_{opt}^{-\Delta C-R\alpha}
\end{equation}

i) By combining different equations that define the thermal traits, it is possible to predict linear scaling relationships among the thermal traits. For example, if we combine Eqs. (\ref{eq:min}) and (\ref{eq:max}) it is predicted that the maximum temperature should scale with the minimum. Following this logic, it is possible to predict relationships among all thermal traits. Despite it has been reported that some traits for a given variable (e.g. population growth rate) are related among them \parencite{rosso1993unexpected}, it remains to be studied whether all traits are related and whether this is conserved or not for different variables. 

ii) It can also be predicted relationships among $Y(T_{opt})$ and traits, e.g. $T_{opt}$. Despite there is evidence for a relationship between $Y(T_{opt})$ and $1/T_{opt}$ and $1/T_{ran}$ in some taxa \parencite{huey1984jack}, it remains an open question whether this is true for all traits and in different taxa. \\
iii) Regarding the most classical meaning or scaling in biology, the change of a biological rate or quantity with the size of a system (e.g. mass, volume, area). One way to derive this is from our averaging approach, where the size is contained in the $Y_0$ term. So, if Y (e.g. species richness) and T (average temperature of a given area) remain constant, but the area of an ecological community changes, then it is expected that the thermodynamic parameter would change with different areas. There are empirical relationships in the literature, of relationships between thermal traits (e.g. minimum, range) and body size \cite{leiva2019scaling,fuller2016towards,peralta2021heat,riek2013allometry} or thermal conduction-related quantities and size \cite{mortola2013thermographic,kwak2016model}. As far as we know there is no theory for the documented relationships between thermal traits and body size. We presume that our model could shed light on some of the principles that could originate those patterns. 
% In this sense predictions made by physical constraint might be distinguishable from other models such as evolutionary, for example. On the other hand, there is a relationship between size and temperature. For example, there is a well-known relationship between (adult) body size and temperature \cite{angilletta2003temperature,zuo2012general} (the so-called temperature-size rule). This relationship might be a basis for deriving relationships between thermal traits and size. 
Besides theory development, more empirical studies are needed as most of these studies focus on a particular group (e.g. ectotherms) but not across distant-sized groups, e.g. bacteria and mammals.

\end{framed}





