\subsection{Initial Conditions}
\label{InitialConditions}

Our calculations model the collapse of a spherical, centrally concentrated molecular gas cloud embedded in a uniform background medium. The cloud has a radius $R_{\rm sphere} = 5.5\,\mathrm{pc} = 5 a_\star$. We adopt an analytic stellar mass of $M_\star = 827\,M_\odot$, rescaled from the model with 25\% SFE introduced by \citet{Bek+2017}, in order to ensure computational feasibility while retaining comparability to the structural properties adopted in previous studies \citep{Bek+2017,bek+2018,bek+2019,2020IAUS..351..507S}.

For comparison, \citet{Bek+2017} define the global SFE within $r < 10a_\star$, as their models exhibit a centrally peaked SFE profile \citep[see Fig.~2 in][]{BekPhDT}.
Within $10a_\star$, the centrally concentrated profile would have a mass of $3260\,M_\odot$.
Our computational box is slightly larger than the spherical cloud. Beyond the cloud we initialize a uniform, initially isothermal background gas at the same temperature $T_{\rm amb} = 20 K$ and density as the surface of the cloud, in order to prevent artificial pressure gradients at the cloud boundary. 
As a consequence of the truncation and embedding in a uniform medium, the actual mass in the box is $3597\,M_\odot$, $\sim10$\% larger than the mass of the full profile used in \citet{Bek+2017}.  For the centrally concentrated density profile we consider, the error from this approximation will be small.

The adopted physical parameters of the cloud are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:Init1}. The initial cloud mass is $M_{\rm gas} = 2513\,M_\odot$.
The cloud is initialized in a state of virial equilibrium with a virial parameter of \(\alpha_{\rm vir} = 0.5\). The minimum allowed resolution in the low-density outer regions corresponds to $16^3$ grid cells, yielding a maximum cell size of $\Delta x_{\rm max} = 0.859\,\mathrm{pc}$. 

We use outflow boundary conditions, but also allow gas to flow into the grid from ghost zones if the boundary velocity is inward. This prevents vacuums from forming at the boundaries.

\subsection{Models}
\label{subsec:models}
We performed a series of simulations at different refinement levels to explore the influence of numerical resolution and stochastic IMF sampling on cluster formation. The corresponding numerical parameters for each simulation are listed in Table~\ref{tab:deluxesplit}. The refinement level $n$ was varied between $n = 3$ and $n = 6$, with maximum effective resolutions ranging from $64^3$ to $512^3$ cells. The minimum cell size $\Delta x_{\rm min}$, sink accretion radius $r_{\rm sink}$, and sink formation threshold $\rho_{\rm sink}$ were adjusted accordingly to ensure that the local Jeans length is properly resolved. For the lowest resolution case ($n=3$), we conducted ten independent realizations with different random seeds to capture statistical variations resulting from stochastic IMF sampling. The simulation naming convention is \texttt{nXsY}, where \texttt{X} denotes the refinement level and \texttt{Y} specifies the random seed.

\section{Results} 
\label{sec:Results}

In general, the formation of young star clusters proceeds through three main phases: gravitational collapse of the gas, star formation, and stellar feedback. However, in realistic environments, these phases overlap. Furthermore, even under identical initial conditions, random variations in the masses of the stars drawn from the IMF and the rotation of the radiation directions can cause the duration of star formation to vary in our models. This raises a fundamental question: what governs the variation in star formation timing and efficiency under identical initial conditions?

\subsection{Impact of Massive Stars}
\label{subsec:ImpactMS}

In this subsection, we investigate the impact of the most massive stars on SFE and cluster structure. We quantify stellar feedback by measuring the expelled gas mass, \( M_{\mathrm{loss,fb}} \), and examine the dependence of SFE on the star formation timescale, \( t_{\mathrm{sf}} \).


