In summary, our results indicate that the total number and mass of stars formed are converging moderately well. However, higher-resolution simulations yield more realistic ionized zones, stellar feedback, gas motion, and accretion flows.

\subsection{Cluster Structure}
\label{sec:plummer_fit}

In this subsection, we compare the density profiles and morphologies of our clusters with the analytically predicted, spherically symmetric, Plummer density profile \(\rho_\star(r)\) with a Plummer radius of $a_\star = 1.1$~pc (see Fig.~\ref{fig:prfle}, blue line). We also examine the dependence of the spatial distribution of the most massive stars on the measured Plummer radius and velocity dispersion. Finally, we compare our results with observational data to evaluate their consistency with observed star cluster properties.

Figure~\ref{fig:Prfle_b} shows  stellar density profiles at \(4\,t_{\rm ff}\). We compare Plummer profiles fit to our computed star cluster density profiles to the analytically predicted Plummer profile for our initial gas distribution. We used a nonlinear least-squares fit to derive the radius and normalization of our Plummer profile fits. To avoid artificial central peaks in the profiles caused by individual massive stars, the innermost region from $0.01$ to $0.2\,\mathrm{pc}$ is excluded from the fit, depending on the simulation.

The fitted Plummer radii provide a quantitative measure of the cluster concentration. As the mass of the most massive stars decreases, the Plummer radius, which reflects the concentration of stars, also becomes smaller (e.g., compare panels \texttt{n3s1} with \(M_{\rm max} = 52\,M_{\odot}\) and \(a_{\rm fit} = 0.43\,\rm pc\) versus \texttt{n3s3} with \(M_{\rm max} = 18\,M_{\odot}\) and \(a_{\rm fit} = 0.20\,\rm pc\)). As the refinement level increases, the stellar density profiles become smoother and more centrally concentrated, as indicated by the increased density within 0.1\,pc (e.g., compare \texttt{n3s8}, \texttt{n4s1}, \texttt{n5s1}, and \texttt{n6s1}). Although the predicted analytic Plummer radius $a_\star =  1.1$~pc, the fitted Plummer radii are systematically smaller. This indicates that the stellar clusters in our simulations are more compact than predicted.

