We find that the mass of the most massive star formed substantially influences the structural parameters of clusters.
Figure~\ref{fig:afit} shows how the maximum stellar mass, \(M_{\max}\), correlates with two structural parameters: the fitted Plummer radius \(a_{\text{fit}}\) and the stellar velocity dispersion \(\sigma_v\). We find that \(a_{\text{fit}}\) increases with \(M_{\max}\) while \(\sigma_v\) decreases  with \(M_{\max}\). Clusters whose most massive star is more massive are larger and dynamically colder.  Similar trends were seen in less centrally concentrated clusters by \citet{2023AAS...24110903L}. These trends result primarily from the expulsion of gas in the center of the cluster prior to further star formation by feedback from the most massive star, although dynamical mass segregation can also play a role \citep{polak2024b, laverde-villareal2025}. 

We find that clusters are well fit by Plummer profiles with small radii. This conclusion aligns with observational results of young clusters such as the Orion Nebula Cluster (ONC). \citet{2018MNRAS.473.4890S} showed that within approximately 0.5\,pc, the ONC is dominated by stellar mass rather than gas, and its structure is well fit by a Plummer model with $a_\star \sim 0.36$~pc. Observations also indicate strong central concentrations of clusters embedded in gas filaments in the ONC \citep{2016AJ....151....5M}.

Our results suggest that a centrally concentrated gas distribution under realistic conditions leads to the formation of a more compact stellar cluster than predicted by the analytic model assuming local gravitational collapse. As the mass of the most massive stars increases, stellar feedback becomes stronger, resulting in faster gas expulsion and a more dispersed cluster. Conversely, when the most massive stars are less massive, the gas is removed more gradually, and the resulting cluster remains more centrally concentrated. This trend is quantitatively reflected in the fitted Plummer radii, which decrease as the mass of the most massive stars decreases.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:Conclusion}

In this study, we investigated how massive star formation and gas structure influence the formation and evolution of young star clusters. Using simulations with varying numerical resolution and massive star formation occurrence, we followed the collapse, star formation, and feedback processes to determine their role in regulating cluster properties. We find the following conclusions.

\begin{itemize}
    \item The gas in the central regions has shorter free-fall times, leading to rapid collapse and intense star formation, while gas in the outer regions has longer free-fall times (see Fig.~\ref{fig:prfle}), so it cannot form stars prior to falling in to the center.
    \item As a result, star formation produces rather more centrally concentrated profiles than predicted from the initial gas distribution by the local collapse theory of \citet{2013A&A...549A.132P}.  The star clusters still form with Plummer profiles from stellar dynamics as predicted, but with much shorter scale lengths (see Fig.~\ref{fig:Prfle_b}). 
    \item The formation time and mass of the most massive stars are critical in determining the strength of stellar feedback and shaping the evolution of young star clusters. This extends the results from \citet{2023AAS...24110903L} to more centrally concentrated initial conditions and a range of massive star formation times.
    \item The final SFE is influenced by both the mass of the most massive star formed and the star formation timescale, $t_{\rm sf}$ (see Figs.~\ref{fig:SFE0_25} and~\ref{fig:half}).
    \item Stars form in subclusters along filaments even in the highly centralized initial gas distributions studied here. Early-forming massive stars promote the formation of interacting subclusters (see Fig.~\ref{fig:Fbound}). In some models, the subclusters merge into a single cluster, while in others they survive separately.  The evolution of such subclusters in less centrally concentrated gas was described by \citet{cournoyer-cloutier2023}.
    \item Higher grid resolution enhances the accuracy of gas modeling, which leads to more compact star clusters with more filament formation and initial subclustering, and higher SFE (see Figs.~\ref{fig:cumilative}--\ref{fig:low}).
\end{itemize}

