For tracking the targets by the mobile devices in the coverage of the $L$ APs, the global controller reserves $X_{\rm s}^{\rm I}\in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$ spectrum bands, each having bandwidth $B_{\rm s,0}$, where $X_{\rm s}^{\rm I}$ is an optimization variable. As a result, the total amount of spectrum resources reserved for target tracking by all the mobile devices is $Z_{\rm s}^{\rm I} = X_{\rm s}^{\rm I}\cdot B_{\rm s,0}$. At the beginning of a time slot, a mobile device will enter the sensing mode if not scheduled by the local controller for communication, the probability of which is $1-\rho_{\rm c}$. %Due to the potentially large number of targets and mobile devices, coordinating mobile devices in accessing the sensing spectrum bands for avoiding any interference can result in high signaling overhead.
As shown in Fig.~\ref{antenna_pattern}, similar to~\cite{sensing_uncoordinated_SG}, in each time slot, the mobile device in the sensing mode randomly chooses one of the $X_{\rm s}^{\rm I}$ reserved spectrum bands with equal probability $1/X_{\rm s}^{\rm I}$, and forms a track beam towards one assigned target~\cite{ISAC_scheduling_yuguang}.\looseness=-1% As a result, similar to the conventional analysis approach~\cite{worst_case_reuse_SG_survey}, we evaluate the worst case in the network planning stage.

