\section{Introduction}
Machine-assisted methods for discovering new physical laws of nature, starting from a given background theory and data, have recently emerged, and seem to hold the promise of someday advancing our understanding of the physical world \cite{ai-descartes23, ai-hilbert24}. Given a symbolic regression problem, in other words, some data representing a phenomenon that one wishes to capture a symbolic representation of, two interesting questions emerge: 1) Can a complete and correct background theory be used to improve on the results of the symbolic regression task? 2) Can the presence of a background theory that incorrectly predicts the results of the phenomenon for which we have data, in fact, be helpful in the symbolic regression task? In practice, the presence of the background theory may actually bias us away from finding a good symbolic form.  But this is the problem faced by leading scientists today in the physical sciences, particularly in physics. We have a solid theory that predicts many phenomena very accurately, but fails in some cases (e.g., data on supernova explosions and the rotation of galaxies are at odds with the equations of general relativity, and the observed abundance of lithium is at odds with the equations that we believe govern the strong and weak nuclear forces and nuclear synthesis in the primordial universe).  However, the fact that there are relatively few known theories in the physical sciences has made the training, testing, and benchmarking of systems that utilize the combination of a background theory and data difficult. 

To address these needs, we have developed SynPAT, a system for generating synthetic physical theories comprising (i) a set of consistent axioms, (ii) a symbolic expression that is a consequence of the axioms and the challenge to be discovered, and (iii) noisy data that approximately match the consequence. We also generate theories that do not correctly predict the consequence.  We give a detailed description of the inner workings of SynPAT and its various capabilities. We also report on our benchmarking of several open-source symbolic regression systems using our generated theories and data.

\begin{comment} %older Intro
Automated means for discovering new physical laws of nature, starting from a given background theory and data, have recently emerged and are proving to have great potential to someday advance our understanding of the physical world \cite{ai-descartes23, ai-hilbert24}. However, the fact that there are relatively few known theories in the physical sciences makes the training, testing, and then benchmarking of these systems difficult. %\Lior{ew questions that we may be asked are why not leveraging theories from math, or alternatively, if there are so few of them, maybe we don't need more... not sure I have a good counter-argument}
The present work seeks to remedy this situation by presenting a first instance of a system capable of generating large numbers of consistent synthetic physical theories along with noisy data that are either good fits to the theories or good fits to a subset of the equations (a.k.a. ``axioms'') of the theories. While benchmark sets of equations with companion data sets exist to test standalone symbolic regression systems \cite{egklitz2020BenchmarkingSS, 10.1145/3205455.3205539, la2021contemporary}, there is no current analog for complete physical theories. We use our synthetically generated theories to benchmark three symbolic regression systems against one another, one which is able to take advantage of the theories and two that can leverage only the data. By ``synthetic'' we mean that the generating theories have no a-priori connection to real physical phenomena. We run the following two benchmark studies:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Discovering a consequence of an axiom system: Given variables $\mathbf{v} = \{v_1,...,v_h\}$, derivatives $\boldsymbol{\delta} =\{d_1,...,d_j\}$ and named constants $\boldsymbol{\lambda} = \{\lambda_1,...,\lambda_k \}$, discover an expression $q(\mathbf{v},\boldsymbol{\delta},\boldsymbol{\lambda})$ from data on ${\mathbf{v},\boldsymbol{\delta},\boldsymbol{\lambda}}$ a list of axioms $\{f_1,...,f_n\}$ defined on potentially more variables $\mathbf{V} = \{v_1,...,v_H\}$, derivatives $\boldsymbol{\Delta} = \{d_1,...,d_J\}$, and named constants $\boldsymbol{\Lambda} = \{\lambda_1,...,\lambda_K\}$ for $h\leq H, j\leq J, k\leq K$. %\Lior{we define here the variables, derivatives, constant, axioms, but not the operators and grammar used for expressing the relations. do we want to add those ? }
    \item Correcting inconsistencies in axioms: With the same notation as above, given an axiom system $\{f_1,...,f_n\}$ that is inconsistent with data on $\mathbf{V},\boldsymbol{\Delta}, \boldsymbol{\Lambda}$, find a replacement axiom $f_i^*$ or $f_i$ (where the problematic %\Lior{do we want to use problematic ? or inconsistent ?} 
    $f_i$ is not a-priori known) so that $\{f_1,...,f_i^*,...,f_n\}$ is consistent and predictive of the data. 
    %\item Correcting theories to explain phenomena: Given an axiom system $\{f_1,...,f_r\}$ and a consequence $q$ that is not implied by the axioms, replace one of $f_1,...,f_r$ in order for $q$ to be derivable from the axioms.
\end{itemize}
\end{comment}

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In the next section, we review related work on symbolic regression, %\Lior{do we focus on symbolic regression or symbolic discovery ?} 
the benchmarking of symbolic regression systems, and recent work employing theories in conjunction with data to discover new scientific laws. Following that, we give a detailed description of our theory, consequence, and data generation processes. We then discuss the case where some portion of a theory is not such a good match to the data and the generation of what we call theories with biased data. Following this, we review the benchmarks we have run using our theories, discuss code and data set availability, and end with conclusions and future work.

\section{Related Work}

Symbolic regression refers to the idea that given some data associated with a set of variables, one can search through a space of mathematical expressions to come up with a symbolic expression from the search space that best characterizes the data in the sense of minimizing a given error metric. This idea in its full generality dates back to 1992 %\Lior{Pat Langley's Bacon work dates back to 1981, and possibly it is not the first, I have added the bibtex to th reference.bib file, under langley1981bacon, if you want to add it in} 
and the textbook on genetic programming \cite[Ch. 2.4]{koza1992}. However, the BACON.5 system of \cite{langley1981bacon}, which discovered physical conservation laws from a small set of data-driven heuristics, dates back much further -- to 1981. Linear and multilinear regression date back far earlier with the discovery of linear regression actually being credited to Newton \cite{GROPING16}.  

Some of the early work on symbolic regression includes \cite{korns06, duffy99, bautu95} for discovering individual equations not involving derivatives, and then the work of Bongard and Lipson \cite{bongard-pnas07}, which allows not just for the discovery of individual equations with ordinary derivatives among the regressed symbols but \emph{coupled systems} of such equations. %A limitation of the Bongard and Lipson framework, however, is that all variables in all equations must be observable/measurable - a limitation that does not exist in other work. 
Given some data and the problem of finding one or more best-fitting governing ordinary differential equations, with initial conditions either provided or output as part of the solution, several recent approaches have utilized deep neural networks and even transformers. There is a rapidly growing literature, but some examples include NODE \cite{NODE2018}, ODEFormer \cite{d'ascoli2024odeformer}, which comes with a dataset of 63 %\Lior{should we mention the number ?since it is nit relative to another reference number, not sure it provides meaningful gauge} \jon{I have included the number because we also provide a number for the subsequent Feynman dataset.} 
ODEs from the literature that the authors refer to as ODEBench, and the predecessor system to ODEFormer \cite{10.5555/3618408.3618491}. Both ODEFormer \cite{d'ascoli2024odeformer} and its predecessor system borrow ideas from \cite{cava2018learning}, which originated the concept of learning concise representations by evolving networks of trees of operators. A nice comparison of some of the most important symbolic regression methods and systems up through 2021 is given in \cite{la2021contemporary}, which also includes a large benchmark and benchmark dataset (to be discussed later). %\Joao{should this be: which also includes a large benchmark dataset?} \jon{They provide not only the results of their benchmark but the dataset they use for the benchmarking.}

At least two commercially available symbolic regression (SR) systems exist: Eureqa (software that is now part of the DataRobot software suite)%\footnote{https://docs.datarobot.com/en/docs/modeling/analyze-models/describe/eureqa.html}
, the algorithm behind which is described in \cite{doi:10.1126/science.1165893}, and the Wolfram SR system,  described in \cite{wolfram2004}. Both Eureqa and Wolfram use genetic algorithms. The Eureqa system was judged to be state of the art by the AI Feynman system \cite{doi:10.1126/sciadv.aay2631, udrescu2020aifeynmanphysicsinspiredmethod}, which is one of the three systems we describe the benchmarking of, using SynPAT, in the present work. 

The AI Feynman system is a ``physics-inspired'' symbolic regression system. It recursively applies a set of decomposition tactics and specialized solvers gleaned from the study of known physics equations, followed by polynomial fitting and brute-force search. If the problem is not directly solved, it appeals to a deep neural network (NN) based component. As usual with most %(but not all symbolic regression systems -- see the systems AI Descartes and AI Hilbert %\Lior{do we mean to mention them as exceptions? if we do, we may want to make it clearer} 
%that we discuss shortly), 
symbolic regression systems, it starts with data and tries to discover a single best governing \emph{function}, given user-specified variables and constants, each of which has associated units of measure. It does not support derivatives. %Note that some SR systems do not require that the governing equation be a function (it could be, say, a two or higher dimensional surface). AI Feynman makes use of six conceptual observations about physics equations that it uses in both its decomposition/pre-NN stage and also as part of its NN component. Some of the most important of these observations include the fact that equations in physics have a strong tendency to be dimensionally consistent (so that every term has the same aggregate unit of measure), the functions tend to either be low-order polynomials or be composed of low order polynomials  components, and they tend to have a certain compositionality, meaning they tend to be a composition of a small set of elementary functions, each typically taking no more than two arguments.
\begin{comment}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Units} -- the provided units of the variables and constants enable it to effectively do so-called dimensional analysis and strongly favor output functions that are dimensionally consistent, in other words functions that have the same aggregate unit of measure in every term. 
\item \emph{Low Order Polynomials} -- the discovered function tends to either be a low-order polynomial or at least part of it is a low-order polynomial.
\item \emph{Compositionality} -- the discovered function tends to be a composition of a small set of elementary functions, each typically taking no more than two arguments.
\item \emph{Smoothness} -- the discovered function is generally continuous and often analytical (having derivatives of all orders) over the range of values for which data is provided.
\item \emph{Symmetry} -- the discovered function tends to exhibit various symmetries with respect to its variables.
\item{Separability} -- the discovered function can often be written as a sum or product of two parts with no variables in common.
\end{enumerate}
\end{comment}

%With these observations at its core, 
The AI Feynman system was able to use a feedforward neural network with 6 hidden layers, and using 100,000 synthetically generated exactly matching data points per equation, recover all 100 of the equations it collated from the Feynman Lectures on Physics, volumes 1--3 \cite{feynman1963}. The results were compared to a 71\% score on the same test set for the Eureqa SR system. As confessed by the authors, there is an inherent mixing of training and test data in these results, so they actually treat these 100 equations as training data for a further test on what they call 20 ``bonus'' equations culled from a variety of additional classical physics textbooks. The AI Feynman system was not further tweaked for these additional equations, but, again in response to 100,000 exactly matching synthetically generated data points per equation, the system was able to recover 90\% of the equations versus just 15\% when running Eureqa. 

Following the tests on exact data, the AI Feynman system was also run on data generated with systematic multiplicative Gaussian noise applied to the function value starting at a multiplicative value of $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ and increasing by powers of 10 until each equation could no longer be rediscovered. Noisy data results were just reported for the 100 Feynman Lecture series problems. The minimum value of $\epsilon$ for which each problem is still solvable is reported, with the authors remarking that ``most of the equations can still be recovered exactly with an $\epsilon$-value of $10^{-4}$ or less, while almost half of them are still solved for $\epsilon = 10^{-2}$''.

A second system we benchmark in this work is PySR \cite{pysr, tonda25}. Unlike AI Feynman's physics-inspired approach which uses heuristics such as dimensionality analysis, compositionality, and symmetry detection, PySR is designed to be a generalized, physics-agnostic, symbolic regression model that uses genetic programming and maintains diverse populations of equations that evolve in parallel. 

The final symbolic regression package we benchmark in this work is the recent system known as AI Hilbert \cite{ai-hilbert24}. Both AI Hilbert and its predecessor system, AI Descartes \cite{ai-descartes23}, solve a fundamentally different problem from that of the other symbolic regression systems we have considered thus far. These systems consider the case where one is given a physical theory represented by a set of equations, or what they call ``axioms'', together with some potentially noisy data in a subset of the same variables as the theory. They  then attempt to find a consequence of the theory that (i) is a good fit to the data, (ii) is as faithful to the theory as possible, while (iii) favoring simplicity of expression of the generated consequence.
%, new axioms, or try to find a new axiom (or axioms) that better matches (match) the data than one or more of the existing axioms. %\karan{Regarding the previous sentence: AI Hilbert is reported to only find a consequence of the theory that minimizes the error on data regularized by distance to the background theory. The original paper and experiments make no mention of discovering axioms with it.} \Joao{I think this is also true for AI Descartes.} 
In AI Hilbert, the theories and generated consequences are limited to equations that are multivariate polynomials in the variables and constants provided. So, e.g., exponentials and trigonometric functions are not supported and neither are derivatives. Unlike other SR systems, AI Hilbert and its predecessor system make use of linear and non-linear optimization methods and theorem proving. When a new consequence of a given theory is discovered that effectively explains some data, a proof of derivability is also generated.

%Several of the results shown in \cite{ai-hilbert24} demonstrate that AI Hilbert can derive known consequences of existing theories from those theories, e.g., their derivation of the Hagen-Poiseuille equation and the Radiated Gravitational Wave Power equation -- this latter being one of the few ``bonus'' equations featured in the AI Feynman paper \cite{feynman1963} in which neither AI Feynman nor Eureqa could find the correct equation -- though in the cases of AI Feynman and Eureqa the derivations were from data, not theory. An additional result shows that starting with a set of axioms consistent with special relativity, plus one axiom from Newtonian mechanics that is \emph{not} valid relativistically (and not consistent with the other axioms), together with data that is consistent with special relativity and characterizes how two observers in relative motion to one another at near light speeds experience time, the AI Hilbert system is able to reject the erroneous axiom and in turn derive the relativist time dilation law, which correctly characterizes the data. A similar derivation is made for Kepler's Third Law of Planetary Motion in the presence of an inconsistent background theory and  data. Finally, the AI Hilbert paper considers the case where the axiom system is incomplete and inadequate to derive Kepler's Third Law, and they investigate how many data points are required to recover the Third Law. The authors note that the AI Hilbert system includes a metric to assess the distance of a discovered equation from the incomplete background theory. %\Lior{don't know whether it is useful to mention that AI Descartes offers a metric to assess the distance of a discovered relation from partial body of background theory, it may be implied that AI Descartes and Ai Hilbert replies on having complete theory, which is not the case}




Several symbolic regression benchmarking data sets comprising varying numbers of individual equations and accompanying data have been compiled over the course of time.  We have already mentioned the 100 equations from the Feynman lectures and 20 ``bonus'' equations from leading physics text books, along with 100,000 synthetically generated data points, mentioned in \cite{udrescu2020aifeynmanphysicsinspiredmethod} and available at \cite{f-srdb2020}. A small set of non-linear first-degree ordinary differential equations gleaned from Strogatz's textbook on nonlinear dynamics and chaos \cite{strogatz2000}, together with accompanying data, has been compiled in the so-called ODE-Strogatz repository \cite{stro-repo2020}. %Besides the fact that the first set of equations are not differential equations, a key point  is that they are \emph{static} so that the data does not evolve over time or over some other one (or more) independent variables. While, in the Strogatz case the data do have this property.
Additional compilations include \cite{egklitz2020BenchmarkingSS}, which includes several synthetic equations as well as equations from a variety of engineering disciplines, and \cite{10.1145/3205455.3205539}, which pulled 94 real-world regression equations from the Penn Machine Learning Benchmark (PMLB) \cite{PMLB17}. Finally, it is important to mention the compilation known as SRBench \cite{la2021contemporary}, which assembled 252 SR regression equations for use in a large benchmark of SR and machine learning methods. All the equations from this study were imported into the previously mentioned Penn Machine Learning Benchmark (PMLB). 
We believe we are the first to provide benchmarks that combine not just single equations with data but entire physical theories with data. Having this latter type of benchmark is important to see whether systems that incorporate entire or partial theories in addition to data, like AI Descartes \cite{ai-descartes23} and AI Hilbert \cite{ai-hilbert24}, perform noticeably better than systems that appeal to data alone for various regression tasks.

