Finally, we mention the very recent article \cite{constantin2024statisticalpatternsequationsphysics}, which is neither a symbolic regression paper nor a symbolic regression benchmark paper per se, but rather an article that makes some observations about statistical patterns among physics equations. The authors study three sets of equations: the collection of 100 equations from the Feynman lectures,  a list of equations found on Wikipedia named after people \cite{enwiki:1249228356}, and another collection whose equations try to explain various aspects of the expanding universe \cite{MARTIN201475}. The authors look at the prevalence of different operators (addition, multiplication, exponentiation, taking of logarithms, and so forth) among these equations and find that if you rank order them in terms of their commonality, their relative frequency tends to follow the Zipf-Mandelbrot Law:
\begin{equation*}
    f(r) \sim \frac{1}{(r + \beta)^\alpha},
\end{equation*}
for appropriate constants $\alpha, \beta$, and $\alpha$ very close to $1$. Zipf-Mandelbrot is the two-parameter variant of Zipf's Law, $f(r) \sim \frac{1}{r^\alpha}$. In linguistics, for example, where the law is commonly observed to hold, this says that the most common word in a language is approximately twice as common as the second most common word, and three times as common as the third most common word, and so on. So too, but with the slightly different Zipf-Mandelbrot Law, in the case of the operators in physics. A problematic aspect of their conclusion, while elegant, is that while the Feynman equations and the Wikipedia list of equations named after people both observe a similar Zipf-Mandelbrot frequency relation the ordering of the respective operators is different in the two lists. Moreover, the operator frequency in the inflationary cosmology set of equations follows an exponential distribution a bit better than it does the Zipf-Mandelbrot relation.
