\section{Code and Generated Data Set Availability}\label{CodeAvail}

All code and scripts needed to reproduce the results of this paper, together with generated data sets, and artifacts used to parameterize the SynPAT system (including our analysis of equations from the Feynman database \cite{f-srdb2020}) are freely available under an open source license at \url{https://github.com/jlenchner/theorizer}. The implementation allows for adjustment of the number of variables, constants, derivatives, and equations (as well as their specification). In addition, one can specify variable and derivative value ranges and specify the parameters governing noisy data models. We provide complete documentation of these and more configurable parameters and their effects on theory generation in the repository. A detailed breakdown of the computational resources used in generating the dataset is provided in Appendix \ref{computation_resources}. 

\section{Conclusions and Future Work}

 In future work, we intend to incorporate trigonometric functions into our generated equations -- functions that appear in 18 of the 100 Feynman equations. The input to a trig function is always an angle measured in degrees or radians %(possibly multiplied by a dimensionless quantity) 
 producing, in turn, a dimensionless quantity. For example, for an argument of $\theta$, the result of taking, say, $\sin(\theta)$, is the ratio of an opposite leg to a hypotenuse -- with the units of measure canceling each other out. %Hence, the addition of trigonometric functions into our basic framework does not seem problematic.%, at least for the case where $k$ is a small integer. However, there are a couple of instances where the argument to the trigonometric function is more complex, incorporating integer valued variables (something we do not currently support), half angles, or more complex expressions. 
 There are also 9 equations among the Feynman set that include exponentials. These equations require a detailed dimensional analysis for us to make headway with, something we have not yet done. There are 3 additional problematic Feynman equations: one features $\ln()$, one features the standalone constant 1 as a term, and the other features the $\tanh()$ function. Given the small number of these, their inclusion at present is not a high priority.

Additionally, we have not analyzed the ODE repositories, like \cite{d'ascoli2024odeformer, stro-repo2020}, with the same degree of rigor as we have the Feynman dataset and thus our generation of ODEs is somewhat ad hoc. We leave this and potentially a foray into generating partial differential equations for future work.

Next, it may be that our term-by-term method of generating equations, first estimating and then randomly generating parameters such as the number of terms per equation and the number of factors per term, is not the best approach. In the SR community, it is more popular to think of an equation as a tree of operators \cite{austel2020symbolic}, %This is, for example, how the ODEFormer paper on discovering ordinary differential equations from data using sequence-to-sequence transformers generates its test data. 
a way of unpacking equations that also underlies the AI Feynman architecture. Our way of constructing random equations may therefore implicitly bias \emph{against} systems like AI Feynman. %Although in our system, we have artificially avoided large exponents, they do implicitly occur in the Feynman dataset, especially in relativistic equations where one commonly sees a divisor of the form $\sqrt{1 - v^2/c^2}$. Since we don't deal directly with fractional exponents, nor the composition of operators, we must multiply through by this relativistic divisor to clear the denominator and then square both sides of the resultant equation, which doubles the exponents of all the terms. 
In future work, we intend to also generate systems of equations using operator trees, though we don't at present know how to do this in a principled manner that guarantees dimensional consistency.

Our benchmarking of symbolic regression systems, some with the ability to incorporate an existing theory and some without, is not meant to be an exhaustive benchmarking or comparison of such systems, but rather an example usage of our tools. We will report on more extensive benchmarking at a future time. Finally, it should not be forgotten that there is one use case that we have generated data for that no current system can solve: namely, where we start with a flawed theory, replace a random axiom with random replacement that is not consistent with the original system, generate data for this new system, and ask the regression systems to identify the flawed axiom along with a suitable replacement that better explains the data. This is the holy grail that we think systems that can reason based on theories should be aiming to solve.

