As seen in Table \ref{tab:models-comparison}, AI Hilbert performs best with tolerance to noise, solving $16/22$ ($\sim 72\%$ accuracy) test cases without noise and maintaining 12/22 ($54\%$ accuracy) and 9/22 ($\sim 40\%$ accuracy) success rates at $\epsilon=10^{-3}$ and $\epsilon=10^{-1}$ noise levels respectively. However, we see a drop in performance when axiom systems are replaced with incorrect axiom systems, where the accuracy for each respective noise level drops from $72$\% to $45$\%, $50$\% to $31$\% and $41$\% to $22$\%. This brings the performance close to PySR, which for the varying noise levels of $\epsilon= 0, 10^{-3},$ and $10^{-1}$, we get accuracies of $\sim 41\%, \sim 22\%, $ and $\sim 14\%$ respectively. AI Feynman only achieved accuracies of $\sim 18\%, \sim 13\%, $ and $\sim9\%$ for the varying noise levels. 

We also see in Tables \ref{tab:var-performance}, \ref{tab:deriv-performance}, and \ref{tab:eq-performance}, the most significant drop in performance in PySR and AI Hilbert comes with increasing the number of variables in the axiom system being considered. PySR and AI Feynman are both agnostic of the number of equations in the background theory and do not take them into account during their search. AI Hilbert does take into account the number of equations, but is unaffected by a small change in the number of equations from $4$ to $5$. The tables also suggest that performance is not sharply affected by the number of derivatives, although the proportion of correct solutions does seem to decline with an increase in the number of variables and derivatives present in the axiom system. 

We note some factors that might affect the performance of the models. The first is that these systems were run on different hardware. AI Hilbert and PySR were run on an Apple Macbook Pro M2 system with 8 cores and 16GB RAM on 4 threads, while AI Feynman was run on a high-performance computing node with Intel Xeon Platinum 8260 processors with 192GB RAM on 48 threads. The second is the runtime. We imposed a 2-hour wall-clock timeout constraint on PySR and AI Hilbert. For each attempted solution, AI Hilbert averaged a CPU time of approximately 30 minutes, PySR 1 hour and 15 minutes, and AI Feynman 15 hours. Given that the models were run on different hardware, the CPU time to wall-clock time upper limit (only imposed on PySR and AI Hilbert) is not one-to-one across the models. Therefore, while AI Feynman averaged a wall-clock time of 1 hour and 30 minutes per attempted solution, relatively closer to the wall-clock times of PySR and AI Hilbert - 1 hour and 40 minutes respectively - the CPU time was drastically higher due to the compute resources dedicated to it. When the time constraint is exceeded, for AI Hilbert, we count these as failure cases since these models do not provide intermediate results. Since PySR does provide intermediate results, upon exceeding the time constraint, we consider the best five candidate equations available and compare to the true solution. In our experimentation, when PySR exceeded the time constraint, the available candidates did not correspond to the true solution, and hence were counted as failure cases. Since no time constraint was used for AI Feynman, the failure cases were either the model failing to converge to a solution or converging to the incorrect solution. The third is that each model was run with the default setup publicly available in the respective github repositories associated with the papers. We made no changes to the off-the-shelf AI Feynman architecture and only made hyperparameter changes to the available models. For AI Feynman, we set the \textit{BF try time} - time limit for each brute force call - to 30 seconds, the maximum degree of the polynomial fit routing to 3, the number of epochs for training to 100, and we used the default list of operations in the brute force search (Add (+), Multiply (*), Subtract (-), Divide (/), Increment (>), Decrement (<), Invert (I), sqrt (R)). For AI Hilbert, we configured the model with the Gurobi optimizer (version 9.5) using default tolerance settings and sparsity constraints. For PySR, we used a population size of 400 candidate expressions evolved over 750 generations, with expression complexity limited to 100 nodes and parsimony coefficient of 0.01. These hardware and hyperparameter constraints were chosen to yield comparable results. But it is surely the case that further fine tuning of the neural models as well as of the optimization-based models, along with running on uniform hardware with longer run-times would have affected the results. 


