\section[Centrosymmetry]{Symmetric Centrosymmetric Matrix}\label{sec:rsc}
%=========================================================

An $N\times N$ centrosymmetric matrix, $H$, satisfies the commutation relation 
\begin{align}
	[H, \J ] = 0 \Rightarrow H_{i,j} = H_{i', j'}\;\forall\; i,j,\qquad i' \equiv N+1-i
\end{align}
where $\J\equiv \cbr{\delta_{i, N+1-j}}$ is the exchange matrix.
% Being one of the permutation matrices, $\J$ is often encountered in physics, e.g.~the Pauli matrix $\sigma^x$ for $N = 2$, anti-diagonal Dirac-Lorentz transformation generator for $N = 4$. 
$\J$ is symmetric, invertible ($\J = \J^{-1} = \J^T$), involutory ($\J^2 = \mathbb{I}$, the identity matrix) and has two distinct eigenvalues, $\pm 1$ with multiplicities $\lceil \frac{N}{2} \rceil$ and $\lfloor \frac{N}{2} \rfloor$, respectively. 
% Thus, exchange matrix belongs to a $\mathbb{Z}_2$ symmetry group. 
If $N = 2^L$, then an $N\times N$ exchange matrix can be expressed as $\underset{k}{\otimes} \sigma^x_k$ where $\sigma^x_k$ is the Pauli X matrix in $\hat{\sigma}_z$ representation acting on the $k$th site of a fictitious 1D lattice with $L$ sites. Since $\sbr{\sigma^x, \mathbb{I}} = 0$, any matrix of the form $\sigma^x_i \sigma^x_j\dots \sigma^x_k$ commutes with $\J$ where $1\leq i<\dots < k \leq L$. The exchange matrix also commutes with the matrices of the form $\mathbb{I}^{N_1\times N_1} \oplus \ot\oplus\mathbb{I}^{N_2\times N_2}\oplus \J \ot\J \oplus \mathbb{I}^{N_1\times N_1}$, where $\ot$ is any arbitrary operator of dimension $N - (2N_1+N_2)$.

In addition to centrosymmetry, if a matrix $H$ has diagonal symmetry, 
%(i.e.~$H = H^T$),
then, such a matrix is called symmetric centrosymmetric (SC), which is persymmetric as well~\cite{Cantoni1976}
\begin{align}
	H = \J H^T\J \Leftrightarrow H_{i,j} = H_{j', i'}.
\end{align}
%Any $N\times N$ SC matrix can be partitioned into four non-overlapping blocks, as shown in Table~\ref{tab_sc_forms}.
Since $[H,\J ]=0$, we can reduce $H$ in the basis of $\J $ via a similarity transformation $Q^THQ = H'$ such that $H'$ assumes a block diagonal form. %as shown in Table~\ref{tab_sc_forms}. The particular choice of $Q_1 = -\J , Q_2 = \J $ gives the simplest form of $H'$, containing the diagonal blocks $B_{1,2}$. 
Such a block structure is a consequence of the two disjoint subspaces in the Hilbert space, 
% corresponding to $B_{1,2}$ called 
which are denoted by odd and even sectors, spanned by anti-symmetric ($\ket{\Phi^-}$) and symmetric ($\ket{\Phi^+}$) states with energy levels $E^\mp$, respectively. % (Table~\ref{tab_sc_ev}).

