%A numerical methodology for the study of multiphase flows with thixotropic behavior is proposed based on the use of Smoothed Dissipative
%Particle Dynamics (SDPD). The proposed model is capable of tracking the transient behavior of viscosity while handling the surface tension effects crucial for the study of various physical phenomena present in nature. An extensive validation of the proposed methodology is presented for both static and dynamic cases. In particular, we study static phenomena such as droplet formation and stretching, as well as contact angles on solid surfaces. We also analyze dynamic phenomena including simple and reverse Poiseuille flows, flow around a cylinder, biphasic flows, and the deformation of droplets under shear flow using the Taylor parameter. Additionally, we validate the thixotropic model by comparing the numerical results in a channel with the predictions from the fundamental equations. These results demonstrate the ability of the model to predict interactions between phases in different scenarios. After validating the numerical model, we conduct a numerical exploration of physical phenomena. The first exploration is the analysis of liquid-liquid phase separation (LLPS) in systems formed by proteins immersed in a continuous phase. The thixotropic model allowed to incorporate the time evolution of viscosity due to the progressive formation of aggregates. The results obtained show that the concentration of the protein phase, as well as the inverse capillary number $Ca$, the constitutive parameter $\alpha$ and the thermal fluctuations directly influence the morphology, the distribution and the size of the clusters formed. More specifically, high protein phase concentrations and $Ca$ and low $\alpha$ values promoted the formation of homogeneous, circular and well-defined clusters, while low concentrations and $Ca$ and thermal fluctuations promoted greater heterogeneity in the sizes. The second application explore is the behavior of suspensions and emulsions of non-rigid particles in confined flows considering both continuous Newtonian and thixotropic phases. The rheological behavior of suspensions is validated with the Taylor approximation for deformable particles. The simulations shows a close fit with the evolution of the effective viscosity as the volume fraction of the dispersed phase increases, observing an initially linear behavior and a subsequent parabolic decrease at high concentrations, in agreement with previous observations in the literature. In shear flow experiments with single thixotropic continuous phase, it is verified that the effective viscosity evolves from a maximum value to a limiting viscosity as the internal microstructure of the fluid reorganizes under the imposed stress. This behavior is more evident as the particle volume fraction increased. Poiseuille flow analysis show a progressive decrease in velocity as viscosity increases in low shear stress zones. In the two-phase case, the contrast between the Newtonian continuous phase and the thixotropic dispersed phase generated asymmetric profiles showing the complexity of the interaction between the two phases. Likewise, experiments with emulsions under Poiseuille flow show that those starting with formed microstructure presented less deformation, while emulsions with initially unformed microstructure showed a greater susceptibility to deformation induced by the stress gradient. Other phenomenon analyze is the study of droplet dynamics in a periodically constrained channel, which allowed observing that by varying the thixotropic characteristic time $\lambda_0$, in the case of unformed microstructure, the viscosity increases from its limit value $\eta_\infty$ to the maximum value defined by $\alpha$, requiring fewer cycles of passage through the channel to stabilize when $\lambda_0$ is lower. On the other hand, for the case of formed microstructure, the viscosity starts from its maximum value and decreases until reaching a stable periodic behavior, achieving stabilization for all the analyzed values of $\lambda_0$. In both cases, the local effect of constriction is evidenced, where the viscosity decreases in the constricted zone and increases in the relaxed zone due to abrupt changes in the shear rate. On the other hand, when analyzing the effect of the $\beta$ parameter associated with the destruction rate of the microstructure, it is show that an increase in this parameter amplifies the viscosity variations, while small values of $\beta$ attenuate the effect of shear stress. The last phenomenon studied is the droplet merging through the use of micro devices. The results demonstrate the ability of the numerical approach developed to faithfully capture droplet interaction and melting processes in complex microfluidic configurations. The comparison between the three cases analyzed clearly shows the impact that thixotropic rheology has on the coalescence process. Particularly, the presence of a receiving phase with thixotropic internal structure introduces an additional resistance to the advancement and penetration of the incident droplet, generating a more defined interface and a more ordered internal arrangement.


