\section{Introduction: Historical Perspectives on Observers}
\label{sec:intro}

\begin{quote}
   \noindent \textit{“The observer, when he seems to himself to be observing a stone, is really, if physics is to be believed, observing the effects of the stone upon himself. Thus science seems to be at war with itself: when it most means to be objective, it finds itself plunged into subjectivity against its will.”}

   \hfill -- Bertrand Russell, An Inquiry into Meaning and Truth (London: George Allen \& Unwin, 1940), 14–15.
\end{quote}
%DR-provide full source for this quotation

The concept of an \emph{observer} has been a core puzzle from ancient philosophy to modern physics, shaping how we interpret measurement, reality, and knowledge. Classical thinkers such as Aristotle placed the observer as a passive spectator to the natural world. But the beginning of modern science came with the demand for the observer to take a more active role, as expressed by Francis Bacon in his New Organon (98th Aphorism Concerning the Interpretation of Nature):

\begin{quote}
In the business of life, the best way
to discover a man’s character, the secrets of how his mind
works, is to see how he handles trouble. In just the same way,
nature’s secrets come to light better when she is artificially
shaken up than when she goes her own way.
\end{quote}

In modern physics -- with the stark differences between reference frames in Einstein’s relativity and with Heisenberg's Uncertainty Principle -- the active and constraining role of the observer came to be seen as not just expedient, but as unavoidable \cite{heisenberg1927,einstein1905}.

%Over time, this shift in viewing the observer as an integral participant rather than a mere bystander has profoundly influenced scientific methodology itself. Empirical protocols once presumed purely objective now acknowledge the observer’s theoretical and instrumental framework as part of the measurement process. From the early positivist view that experiments directly reveal “facts” to more contemporary perspectives recognizing that observers inevitably frame and even shape the data collected, scientific revolutions (e.g., Copernican, Newtonian, Einsteinian, quantum) reflect progressive acceptance of the observer’s active role. This acceptance informs not only how experiments are designed and interpreted, but also how scientific theories are evaluated for coherence and explanatory power \cite{kuhn1962}.

In quantum mechanics, the observer problem became explicit, ultimately raising questions about consciousness, apparatus, and whether physical law alone dictates the outcome of measurements \cite{vonNeumann1955, Wheeler1989}. As measurement seemingly “collapses” the wavefunction, intervention was recognized as a prerequisite of observation.  In the field of cybernetics, a complementary development took place: observation was clearly recognized as a prerequisite for intervention, as observation (of outcomes) provides the input to adjust the actions of an agent in a feedback loop \cite{wiener1948, ashby1956}. A similar development can be observed in the fields of biology \cite{maturana1980autopoiesis}, and artificial intelligence \cite{newellSimon1976}, where the observer is increasingly regarded as a feedback-driven agent or subsystem with the capacity to influence and be influenced by its environment.

This paper aims to consolidate these diverse threads into a generalized observer theory. Specifically, we propose a \emph{formal} definition of minimal observers, discuss their philosophical significance, and explain which concepts (such as measurement, internal vs.\ external distinctions, and emergent complexity) hinge on the presence of observers. We also connect these ideas to pressing questions in quantum gravity and digital physics \cite{smolin2019, wolfram2002newkind, ruliology2023, arsiwallaPregeo2023}, where the ultimate structure of reality and computation may depend on observer-oriented frameworks. In addition, we point to dual-aspect monism arguments \cite{atrick} and debates on consciousness that further highlight how observer-internal processes and external meaning may be intertwined.

%DR - explain what the minimal observer notion refers to: I assume the abstracted, essential features of an observer?

Furthermore, by a \emph{minimal observer}, we refer to the simplest possible entity that exhibits the core characteristics of observation: the ability to perceive external states, update internal configurations based on input, and generate an action or output, thereby forming a closed feedback loop. This definition, which we formalize in the following sections, ensures that the observer remains functionally distinct from its environment while engaging in meaningful interactions that shape both its perception and responses.


To make our account as rigorous as possible, we include new mathematical developments: theorems on \emph{observer equivalence} and \emph{observational complexity}, explicit \emph{diagrams} to clarify feedback loops, and in-depth comparisons with established theories in physics and philosophy.

