\section{Minimal Observation Models in Cybernetics}
\label{sec:cybernetics}

\subsection{Introduction to Cybernetics and Observation}

Cybernetics, as pioneered by Norbert Wiener \cite{wiener1948} and developed by Ross Ashby \cite{ashby1956}, is the study of communication and control in organisms and machines. Its hallmark is the \emph{feedback loop}, in which a system senses some variable, processes that information, and acts upon its environment, thus influencing future sensory input. Figure~\ref{cyberneticLoop} (below) shows a simple diagram of this loop, highlighting the observer’s role.

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.65\textwidth]{fig1.png}
\caption{\centering Minimal Observer-Environment Feedback Loop}
\label{cyberneticLoop}

\end{figure}

\subsection{The Sensor-Actuator Feedback Loop}

A simplified cybernetic observer consists of three essential components:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Sensor:} Detects environmental or internal states (e.g., temperature, light intensity).
    \item \textbf{Processing Unit:} Interprets sensor data, often by comparing it to a goal or reference.
    \item \textbf{Actuator:} Executes an action that changes either the system itself or its environment.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Examples of Minimal Cybernetic Observers}

\paragraph{Thermostat} 
A \emph{thermostat} maintains temperature by comparing a set-point to the current temperature sensor reading and toggling a heater. Despite its simplicity, it is widely regarded as a minimal observer system: it senses (temperature), processes (comparing to a set-point), and acts (turning heating on/off). \footnote{Notably, from the perspective of Tononi's \emph{Integrated Information Theory} (IIT), even such a minimal feedback system could be considered to have an extremely rudimentary form of consciousness, as it integrates information (the sensed temperature) and produces a differentiated response (heating action)---albeit at a very low level of complexity or $\Phi$, IIT’s measure of consciousness \cite{tononi2012integrated}.}


%DR - maybe a footnote on Tononi's integrated information theory, and how he would classify this system as having a minimal consciousness level?


\paragraph{Simple Reactive Agents (Braitenberg Vehicles)}
\emph{Braitenberg vehicles} \cite{braitenbergVehicles} demonstrate how purely reactive sensor-actuator links can yield emergent “intelligent-looking” behaviors. Although lacking complex cognition, they meet basic observer criteria by receiving sensory data, updating motor outputs, and influencing their environment in a feedback loop.

