\section{Second-Order Cybernetics}
\label{sec:secondOrder}

\subsection{Overview}

First-order cybernetics keeps the observer \emph{outside} the system: it is a 3rd-person perspective. In \emph{second-order cybernetics} \cite{vonFoerster1974}, the observer is integrated \emph{into} the system, allowing for self-reference, thus giving not just a 1st-person perspective, but one in which the observer enters their own domain.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{fig2.png}
\caption{First-Order vs. Second-Order Cybernetics: (left) First-order observer observes the system. (right) Second-order observer modifies the observation process itself.}
\label{fig:secondOrderDiagram}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Key Concepts}

\textbf{Observer Inclusion:} The observer is part of the feedback process, not a neutral external vantage point. \textbf{Self-Reference:} The observer can observe and modify its own rules, leading to learning or adaptation. \textbf{Constructivism:} Reality emerges through the observer’s activities and interpretations \cite{maturana1980autopoiesis, varela1979principles}.

\paragraph{Example: Learning Neural Networks}
A neural network updating its weights $W$ in response to error signals exemplifies second-order cybernetics. The network \emph{observes} (via a loss function) the mismatch between outputs and targets, adjusting $W$ to reduce future mismatch—thus reconfiguring its own internal parameters.

