\section{Developing a Meta-Model for Observers}
\label{sec:metaModel}

\subsection{Core Components of the Meta-Model}

Across many examples, we identify the following recurring features:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item {Sensing Mechanism (Perception)}
    \item {Processing Unit (Interpretation)}
    \item {Response Mechanism (Action)}
    \item {Feedback Loop (Adaptation)}
    \item {Internal Model or Representation (Prediction)}
    \item {Boundary Definition (Self vs. Environment)}
    \item {Self-Monitoring (Self-Observation)}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{A Formal Definition of Minimal Observers}

%%%%%%%%%%% 

%%%%%%%%%%%

\begin{definition}[Minimal Observer]
\label{def:minObs}
Let $O$ be a system described by the tuple
\[
O = (X, Y, Z, f, g, \mathcal{B}),
\]
where:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[] $X$: Internal state space (finite or countably infinite). 
    \item[] $Y$: Input (sensor) space.
    \item[] $Z$: Output (action) space.
    \item[] $f: X \times Y \to X$: State transition function.
    \item[] $g: X \to Z$: Output function.
    \item[] $\mathcal{B}$: A boundary condition demarcating “inside” (the observer’s internal states) vs. “outside” (the environment).
\end{enumerate}
Then $O$ is \emph{minimal} if:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[] $|Y| \ge 1$ (non-trivial sensing),
    \item[] $|Z| \ge 1$ (non-trivial action),
    \item[] $|X| > 1$ (non-trivial internal dynamics),
    \item[] Feedback closure: the observer’s actions $g(x)$ alter the environment, which in turn alters subsequent inputs $y \in Y$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\paragraph{What Does ``Minimality'' Mean Here?}

This definition positions the minimal observer as the simplest system capable of observation in a functional sense: it must sense ($Y$), update its state ($f$), and act ($g$), with a boundary ($\mathcal{B}$) and feedback loop ensuring interaction with an environment. This is minimal in a structural sense---it avoids complexity like memory, learning, or self-awareness, focusing on the bare essentials of observation. However, ``minimality'' could be interpreted in multiple ways: \textbf{Structural Minimality}, referring to the fewest components needed for observation (e.g. the thermostat example below (Section 5.3) fits structural minimality: it’s a basic feedback system with no self-modification or self-production this approach); \textbf{Functional Minimality}, denoting the simplest system that still performs a meaningful role (e.g., distinguishing internal vs.\ external states); and \textbf{Ontological Minimality}, which identifies the foundational unit from which all observer-like phenomena emerge (possibly tying to autonomy or autopoiesis).


\subsection{Thermostat as a Formal Example}

As an illustration, let $X=\{\text{ON}, \text{OFF}\}$, $Y=\{\text{Cold}, \text{Hot}\}$, $Z=\{\text{HeaterOn}, \text{HeaterOff}\}$. Define
\[
\begin{aligned}
    f(\text{OFF}, \text{Cold}) &= \text{ON}, \quad & f(\text{OFF}, \text{Hot}) &= \text{OFF}, \\
    f(\text{ON}, \text{Cold}) &= \text{ON}, \quad & f(\text{ON}, \text{Hot}) &= \text{OFF}.
\end{aligned}
\]
and
\[
g(\text{ON})=\text{HeaterOn}, \quad g(\text{OFF})=\text{HeaterOff}.
\]
\newline
A boundary $\mathcal{B}$ physically partitions the controller from ambient air. This meets minimal observer criteria: the thermostat senses “Cold/Hot,” toggles $\{\text{ON}, \text{OFF}\}$, and acts by turning heat on/off.

